    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A mower blade does not cut grass the way a knife cuts a tomato. It travels at somewhere near 200 miles per hour at the tip and severs the grass by impact, and that impact is what dulls it — every blade of grass, every grain of sand, every pine cone. The interval between sharpenings is really a question about how much abrasive material your lawn contains.
        </p>

        <div id="interval" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Interval, in Hours and Mowings</h2>
          <p>
            The standard answer is every 20 to 25 hours of run time. For a typical quarter-acre suburban lawn taking about 45 minutes a week, that is roughly every 25 to 30 mowings — realistically two or three sharpenings across a season, which most people handle as spring, midsummer and a final touch-up before winter storage.
          </p>
          <p>
            The variables move that number a long way in both directions.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left min-w-[560px]">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Conditions</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Interval</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per Season</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clean lawn, no debris, loam soil</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 times</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical suburban lawn</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20-25 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 times</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sandy soil or coastal</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-15 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-6 times</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wooded lot, sticks and cones</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-15 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-6 times</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acreage or rough cutting</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8-12 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6+ times</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Any hard strike overrides the schedule. Hitting a rock, a root, an irrigation head or a buried stump chips the edge instantly, and a chipped blade cuts worse than a merely dull one because the damage is concentrated. It also throws the blade out of balance, which is a mechanical problem rather than a cosmetic one. If you hear a strike, stop and look.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical approach that works for most people is to own a second blade. Sharpening a spare on the bench and swapping it in takes ten minutes, which removes the main reason people put this off — nobody wants to interrupt mowing to file an edge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Signs a Blade Has Gone Dull</h2>
          <p>
            The lawn tells you before the blade does. Look at the grass a day after mowing rather than inspecting the edge.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Frayed, Whitish Tips</h3>
          <p>
            Kneel down and look at individual blades of grass. A sharp mower leaves a clean, flat, green cut. A dull one leaves shredded ends that dry out and turn white or tan within a day, which is why a freshly mowed lawn can look slightly brown from a distance.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Grass Standing Back Up</h3>
          <p>
            A dull blade pushes grass over and misses some of it entirely, particularly in soft or damp areas. If you find yourself going over the same section twice, or seeing stray uncut blades standing above an otherwise even cut, the edge is gone.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Whole Lawn Browns After Mowing</h3>
          <p>
            The most reliable sign, and the one people misread as drought stress. Thousands of torn tips drying out at once shifts the color of the entire lawn a day or two after each cut, and it resolves immediately once the blade is sharpened.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">More Bogging and More Fuel</h3>
          <p>
            A dull blade takes more power to move grass, so the engine works harder in thick growth and the deck clogs more readily. On a battery mower this shows up as noticeably shorter runtime per charge, which is often the first thing an owner notices.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">And on the Blade Itself</h3>
          <p>
            With the plug wire disconnected, run a gloved finger along the edge. A serviceable blade feels like a butter knife. A dull one feels rounded with no defined edge at all, and often shows a shiny worn strip where the bevel used to be. Nicks larger than about an eighth of an inch mean grinding past them or replacing the blade.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lawn-damage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What a Dull Blade Actually Does to Grass</h2>
          <p>
            This is the part that justifies the effort. Grass is a plant with a wound response, and how it is cut determines how quickly it recovers.
          </p>
          <p>
            A clean cut severs the leaf and the plant seals the wound in roughly a day. A torn cut leaves a ragged, crushed end with far more surface area exposed, which loses moisture for several days and creates an open entry point for fungal pathogens. Lawns cut with dull blades consistently show more disease pressure — dollar spot, leaf spot, rust — and they brown off faster under heat and drought because every plant is losing water through a damaged tip.
          </p>
          <p>
            The cumulative effect over a season is significant. A lawn mowed twenty times with a dull blade has been wounded badly twenty times, and it spends energy on healing that would otherwise go into root development. The visible result is a thinner, paler, more disease-prone lawn that no amount of fertilizer fully compensates for.
          </p>
          <p>
            This interacts with mowing height, which is the other lever most homeowners under-use. Cutting tall — three to four inches for cool-season grasses — shades the soil, encourages deeper roots and reduces weed germination. A sharp blade at the right height is worth more than most fertilizer programs, and it costs nothing but an hour on a bench a few times a year.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The One-Third Rule Still Applies</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Never remove more than a third of the leaf height in one pass, sharp blade or not. Scalping a lawn that got away from you shocks the plants and exposes soil to weed seed, and it also dulls the blade far faster because the mower is chewing through mass it was not designed to handle. Two passes a few days apart beat one aggressive cut.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How to Sharpen a Mower Blade</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Make It Safe First</h3>
          <p>
            Disconnect the spark plug wire on a gas mower, or remove the battery and the safety key on an electric one. Drain or run down the fuel before tipping a gas mower, and tip it with the carburetor and air filter side up so oil does not flood into the cylinder or the filter. On a riding mower, work from underneath on ramps with the machine chocked, or remove the deck entirely.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mark and Remove the Blade</h3>
          <p>
            Mark which face points down with a paint pen before you loosen anything, because reinstalling a blade upside down is a surprisingly common mistake and it will not cut at all. Wedge a block of wood between the blade and the deck to stop rotation, and break the bolt loose with a breaker bar. Penetrating oil helps on a bolt that has not moved in years.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clean, Then Grind</h3>
          <p>
            Scrape the caked grass off both faces so you can see the actual edge and any cracks. Then sharpen with whichever tool you have: a bench grinder is fastest, an angle grinder with a flap disc is the most common, a mill file is slow and gives the best control, and a drill-mounted sharpening stone is a reasonable middle ground.
          </p>
          <p>
            Follow the existing bevel, which is usually around 30 degrees. Work from the inside outward with smooth passes, keep both ends symmetrical by counting strokes, and keep the metal cool — quench frequently in water if you are using a powered grinder. Blueing the steel means you have drawn the temper out of the edge, and that section will go dull almost immediately.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Over-Sharpen</h3>
          <p>
            Aim for butter-knife sharp, and leave a very slight flat rather than a knife-like point. A razor edge on mower steel folds or chips the first time it meets a pebble, so the fine edge you worked for is gone in ten minutes of mowing while a slightly blunter one lasts the whole interval.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Reinstall Properly</h3>
          <p>
            Sail up, cutting edge leading in the direction of rotation, and torque the bolt to the manufacturer's spec — commonly 35 to 70 foot-pounds depending on the machine. An under-torqued blade can shift on impact and shear the crank key; an over-torqued one can seize or strip.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="balance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Balancing, and Why It Matters More Than People Think</h2>
          <p>
            Sharpening removes metal, and removing more from one end than the other leaves the blade heavier on one side. At 3,000 RPM even a small imbalance generates real vibration, and that vibration goes straight into the spindle bearings, the deck and, on a walk-behind, the engine crankshaft. Blade imbalance is a leading cause of premature spindle failure and of crankshaft seal leaks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Checking takes seconds. A cone balancer costs a few dollars — set the blade on it and see which end drops. A nail driven into a wall works nearly as well. Grind a little more off the heavy end, away from the cutting edge, and check again until the blade sits level.
          </p>
          <p>
            While the blade is off, look at the spindle too. Grab the blade and try to move it up and down and side to side; any noticeable play means worn bearings, and putting a freshly sharpened blade on a worn spindle will not fix a vibration problem. On a rider, check the deck belt and the anti-scalp wheels at the same time, since you have already done the hard part of getting underneath.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more thing that is worth doing on the same afternoon: scrape the underside of the deck clean. Built-up grass restricts the airflow that lifts grass upright before the blade reaches it, and a clogged deck makes a sharp blade behave like a dull one. Our roundup of the <a href="/best-self-propelled-lawn-mower/">best self-propelled mowers</a> covers which deck designs shed clippings best.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. When to Replace Instead of Sharpen</h2>
          <p>
            A blade is a consumable. Most take four to eight sharpenings before they should be retired, and a replacement costs a fraction of what a damaged spindle does.
          </p>
          <p>
            Replace when the cutting edge has receded so far that the blade is visibly narrower than a new one, when the sail at the trailing end has worn thin or developed a wavy edge, when there are any cracks at all — particularly around the center hole — or when the center hole has become elongated from a blade that spun on a loose bolt. Deep gouges that would take a quarter inch of grinding to remove also mean it is time.
          </p>
          <p>
            Thinning at the trailing sail deserves particular attention, because that is the part that generates the airflow. A worn sail means poor lift, poor bagging and poor mulching regardless of how sharp the leading edge is, and it is also the part most likely to fail catastrophically. A piece of blade leaving a deck at operating speed is genuinely dangerous.
          </p>
          <p>
            When buying replacements, match the part number rather than the length. Blade thickness, center hole shape, sail geometry and required torque vary between machines, and a nearly-right blade cuts poorly and stresses the spindle. Buying two at once and rotating them is the easiest maintenance habit in lawn care — one is always sharp and on the bench.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="extend" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Making an Edge Last Longer</h2>
          <p>
            Most blade wear is avoidable, and a few habits stretch the interval considerably.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walk the lawn first.</strong> Thirty seconds picking up sticks, stones, dog toys and the sprinkler head someone left out prevents the impacts that do the real damage. On a wooded lot this is the single highest-value habit.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Raise the deck.</strong> Cutting at three and a half inches rather than two keeps the blade away from soil, sand and exposed roots, and it produces a healthier lawn at the same time. Scalping high spots is one of the fastest ways to ruin an edge.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mow dry.</strong> Wet grass clumps, sticks to the deck and forces the mower to work harder, and wet soil throws grit up into the blade path. It also produces a worse cut.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Avoid the edges of gravel drives and mulch beds.</strong> Two feet of overlap into gravel does more damage in one pass than a whole lawn does in a month. Use a string trimmer for those margins.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Store it dry.</strong> Scrape the deck, wipe the blade and give it a light coat of oil before winter. A rusted edge is a dull edge, and pitting from a season in a damp shed takes real metal to grind away in spring. Our guide to the <a href="/best-gas-lawn-mowers/">best gas lawn mowers</a> covers the end-of-season routine that keeps a machine ready for spring.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you sharpen lawn mower blades?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Every 20 to 25 hours of mowing, which for a typical suburban lawn works out to two or three times per season. Sandy soil, sticks, pine cones or acreage cut it to every 10 to 15 hours. A clean lawn cut weekly can stretch closer to 25 hours, and any strike against a rock or root resets the clock immediately.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you know if a mower blade is dull?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look at the grass, not the blade. Dull blades tear rather than cut, leaving frayed white or tan tips that turn the whole lawn slightly brown a day or two after mowing. Other signs are grass standing back up after a pass, uneven stripes of uncut blades, and the mower bogging down in growth it used to handle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How sharp should a mower blade be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About as sharp as a butter knife, not a razor. A blade honed to a fine edge chips and rolls the first time it meets a pebble, and the edge is gone within one mowing. A durable working edge at roughly a 30 degree bevel with a slight flat at the tip cuts cleanly and survives real conditions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you have to balance a mower blade after sharpening?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, always. An unbalanced blade spinning at 3,000 RPM vibrates the whole deck, and that vibration destroys spindle bearings and eventually the crankshaft seal. A cone balancer costs very little. Sharpen, check the balance, and grind more off the heavy end until it sits level.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many times can you sharpen a mower blade?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically four to eight times before the blade should be replaced. Replace it when the cutting edge has receded noticeably, when the sail at the back is worn thin, when there are cracks or deep gouges, or when the center hole has become elongated. A blade is inexpensive next to a deck spindle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you sharpen a mulching blade the same way?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mostly yes, with one caveat: mulching blades have a longer curved cutting edge that wraps further around the blade, and the whole length needs attention rather than just the last few inches. Keep the original bevel angle and do not grind into the curved sail, which is what creates the airflow that keeps clippings suspended.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a dull mower blade hurt the lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, measurably. A torn grass blade loses more moisture than a cleanly cut one and takes longer to heal, which stresses the plant and leaves the wound open to fungal disease. Lawns mowed with dull blades show more disease pressure and brown off faster in heat than the same lawn cut sharp.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">9. Sharpening Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overheating the Steel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Blue discoloration means the temper is gone and that section will dull within one mowing. Quench often and take lighter passes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Balance Check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A few dollars of balancer prevents hundreds in spindle bearings. Vibration is not cosmetic — it is wear happening in real time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reinstalling the Blade Upside Down</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Common, frustrating and entirely preventable. Mark the down-facing side with paint before you loosen the bolt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Grinding a Razor Edge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thin edges chip and roll on the first pebble. Leave a slight flat and the blade holds its cut for the whole interval.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Tipping a Gas Mower the Wrong Way</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oil floods the air filter and the cylinder, and the mower smokes for an hour afterward. Carburetor side up, always.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Mower Blade Sharpening Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dull blade tears grass rather than cutting it, which browns the tips and opens the lawn to disease. The sharpening itself is where most people go wrong.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sharpening to a Knife Edge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A mower blade wants a working edge at roughly 30 degrees, not a razor. A thin edge chips and rolls the first time it meets a stone or a root, and then cuts worse than a properly blunt one. Aim for an edge you could run a finger along without cutting yourself.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Balance Check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Uneven grinding leaves one end heavier, and an unbalanced blade at 3,000 rpm vibrates hard enough to wear out the spindle bearings and crankshaft. Hang the blade on a nail through its centre hole after sharpening — if one end drops, take more off that side.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overheating the Steel on a Grinder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bench grinder can take the temper out of a blade in seconds, and a blue-tinted edge has lost its hardness permanently and will dull almost immediately. Work in short passes, keep the blade cool, and dip it in water regularly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Disconnecting the Spark Plug or Battery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Turning a blade by hand can turn the engine over far enough to fire it. Pull the spark plug lead on a gas mower or remove the battery on an electric one before the mower is tipped, every single time. This is the one mistake in this list that hurts you rather than the mower.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

