        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Sharpen mower blades every 20 to 25 hours</strong> — two or three times a season on a clean suburban lawn, four to six on sandy or wooded ground, and immediately after any hard strike. The lawn tells you before the blade does: frayed white tips and a lawn that browns a day after mowing mean the edge is gone.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Sharpen to butter-knife, not razor. <strong class="text-white">Always balance afterward</strong>, keep the steel cool, and buy a second blade so a sharp one is always ready. It is the cheapest thing you can do that visibly improves how a lawn looks.
          </p>
